WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Hey there, I'm John Nugent, and welcome to LifeMap.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.100
I'm going to answer the question I get asked more than any other…

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.300
how much can I actually borrow?

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.800
Don't worry, we'll keep it simple. And we'll follow a real-life example, so you can see exactly how it works.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.600
Let's meet Aoife and Conor.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.200
They're renting at the moment, paying €1,500 a month…

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.800
and they're dreaming of their own front door.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.000
Between them, they earn €85,000 a year.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.200
So… how much could they borrow?

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.600
Lenders look at five things. Let's take them one by one.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.900
First… loan to income.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.800
As a rule, you can borrow up to four times your combined income.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.300
For Aoife and Conor, that's €85,000 times four… which is €340,000.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.600
Some lenders can stretch a little further for high earners. But you'd need to be approved at four times first.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.400
Second… your deposit.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.000
Every lender asks for at least ten percent of the house price.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.700
So we add that to Aoife and Conor's €340,000.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
And that gives us a house price of €377,778.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:13.900
That's €340,000 to borrow, plus a deposit of about €37,778. That's ten percent of the price.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.800
Now, here's some good news. There's a Help to Buy scheme that can go towards your deposit.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:27.500
It's for first-time buyers of a new home priced under €500,000… and you'll need to have paid income tax and DIRT in Ireland over the last four years.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.300
You can claim the lowest of three things: ten percent of the property value,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.600
the tax you've paid over those four years, or €30,000. Whichever comes out smallest.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.800
Third… net disposable income. That's simply whether you'd have enough left over to live on each month.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.300
Lenders work this out with a formula based on your family size, not your actual spending.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.100
Fourth… your debt servicing ratio.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.900
Lenders limit how much of your income can go on repaying debts, like loans or credit cards.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.100
If you've already got a lot of borrowing, adding a mortgage could push you over that limit.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.200
The good news? Aoife and Conor only have one small loan, so neither of these is a worry for them.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.800
Fifth… repayment capacity.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.300
Lenders need to be sure you can comfortably afford the monthly payment.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:17.900
Over thirty-five years, a mortgage of €340,000 comes to roughly €1,350 to €1,450 a month, depending on the lender.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.300
Some lenders also offer cashback, usually one to two percent when you draw down.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.500
That can make the monthly payment a touch higher… but you get some money back at the start.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.300
Now… Aoife and Conor are paying €1,500 a month in rent, and they're saving €300 a month on top.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.100
That's €1,800 a month they've shown they can manage. And that comfortably covers the repayment.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.200
They could even look at a shorter mortgage term than thirty-five years.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.000
So there you have it. Five checks, and a clear picture of what Aoife and Conor could borrow.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.300
Your own numbers will be different… and that's completely okay. That's what we're here for.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.000
Pop your details into LifeMap, and we'll talk you through your own options.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.600
